+
+## J604 external display coprocessors (dcpext0/1)
+
+The J713 dcpext handoff (aurora-silicon/aura #74, `dt_reserve_j713_dcpext()`)
+reads `dcpextN/iop-dcpextN-nub` `uuid` and `segment-ranges`, the DISPEXTn
+SYS/FE/CPU power states, and the internal DCP handoff. In the J604 25G83
+runtime ADT the dcpext0/1, dart-dcpext0/1 and dart-dispext0/1 nodes have the
+J713's registers, interrupts and segment names (32 of 32 values equal); only
+phandle references differ. On the J604 the handoff needs no configuration
+line: the J604 kernel ships the external display driver enabled.
+`chosen.asahi,j713-dcpext=0` turns it off; the J713 keeps the opt-in `=1`.
+
+The J713 boots its kernel with an OS device tree into which U-Boot imports
+the handoff (aurora-silicon/u-boot #31). The J604 boots the kernel's own
+device tree, completed by m1n1, in which the dcpexts, their mailboxes and
+DARTs and the DISPEXTn power states are disabled. On the J604
+(`T8132_PATH_DCPEXT_ENABLE`), `dt_enable_j604_dcpext()` therefore enables a
+dcpext that carries the marker, with its mailbox, firmware DART, display DART
+and their power-domain chain, in one display transaction. Every power domain
+on that chain must be `apple,firmware-owned` with `#power-domain-cells = <0>`.
+The piodma child stays disabled (the kernel enables PIODMA at runtime). When
+any step fails, the tree is left as it was and the marker is withdrawn, so
+the kernel gate stays closed.
+
+TO-VERIFY on the J604: that the 25G83 iBoot leaves DISPEXTn SYS and FE on and
+the CPU domain on or targeted on (Linux has always found dispext0_sys
+`off-0`, but that reading comes after genpd's unused power-off; m1n1 reads
+PMGR before Linux runs).
